    //\r
    // At grazing incidence a pixel's world footprint is a long thin sliver: one\r
    // axis blows up while the other stays small. \`footprint\` above averages the\r
    // two, so simply tilting the camera down towards the horizon inflates it by\r
    // an order of magnitude — and anything keyed off it fades out, even though\r
    // the surface is no further away and is still perfectly resolvable across the\r
    // sliver's short axis. For the natural detail layers that trade is fine and\r
    // deliberate. For carved snow it is not: it means the trail changes shape\r
